WHICH KEY IT IS BOUND TO

  Fingerprint           {{ .PublicKeyFingerprint }}
  Type                  {{ .PublicKeyType }}

The code only ever produces certificates for that key. `service retrieve`
never resubmits a public key, so a stolen code cannot be paired with someone
else's keypair. If you do not recognize this key, treat the enrollment as
unwanted and expire it.

WHEN IT STOPS WORKING

  Code expires          {{ datetime .CodeExpiresAt }} (in {{ until .CodeExpiresAt }})

Enroll again before then to keep an unattended job running. The code outliving
each certificate is the point: that is what lets a cron job keep renewing
without a human re-approving it.

SETTING THE JOB UP

Add this to the ~/.ssh/config of the account that runs the job, replacing
/path/to/key with the private key you enrolled:

  Match user {{ if .ServiceAccount }}{{ .ServiceAccount }}{{ else }}USERNAME{{ end }} exec 'ssoossh service retrieve --code CODE --key /path/to/key'
    IdentityFile /path/to/key
    IdentitiesOnly yes

`Match user` names the account being connected to on the far side, which for
these certificates is always the service account above: it is the sole
principal, so ssh cannot present the certificate as anyone else.

No CertificateFile line is needed. ssh derives /path/to/key-cert.pub from
IdentityFile's name, which is why the three file names are not negotiable:
the private key is <name>, the public key is <name>.pub, and the certificate
is <name>-cert.pub, all in one directory. Match exec runs before ssh reads
the key files, so this needs no agent.

The certificate is refreshed automatically when it has expired or expires
within a minute. Change that with --grace <time>, or refresh unconditionally
with --force. <time> is a number followed by s, m, or h.

The code is reusable until it expires, so the same command is safe to run
from cron or a systemd timer.

